<!--
  Settings > CrossPoint X3.

  THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR THE DEFAULTS.

  A Settings.bundle needs its defaults stated twice — DefaultValue decides what
  Settings.app DISPLAYS for an untouched key, registerDefaults decides what
  -boolForKey: RETURNS for it, and neither can see the other. Rather than write
  them out twice and hope they stay in step, CrossPointPrefs.mm reads this file
  at launch and builds its registration domain from these DefaultValues.

  So: edit defaults HERE, and only here. Adding another switch needs no code
  change at all — give it a Key and a DefaultValue and it is registered.

  Keys still have to match the ones CrossPointPrefs.mm reads; checkKnown() there
  logs a key that is missing from this file rather than silently answering NO.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>StringsTable</key>
  <string>Root</string>
  <key>PreferenceSpecifiers</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>Type</key>
      <string>PSGroupSpecifier</string>
      <key>Title</key>
      <string>Screen</string>
      <key>FooterText</key>
      <string>CrossPoint holds the screen on while you read. Turn these off to keep it awake instead — useful propped on a stand, where the screen dimming mid-page is the interruption.</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Type</key>
      <string>PSToggleSwitchSpecifier</string>
      <key>Title</key>
      <string>Allow Device to Sleep on Battery</string>
      <key>Key</key>
      <string>allowSleepOnBattery</string>
      <key>DefaultValue</key>
      <true/>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Type</key>
      <string>PSToggleSwitchSpecifier</string>
      <key>Title</key>
      <string>Allow Device to Sleep While Charging</string>
      <key>Key</key>
      <string>allowSleepWhileCharging</string>
      <key>DefaultValue</key>
      <true/>
    </dict>

    <!--
      Button pad contrast.

      The pad's controls are hollow: a one-pixel outline around nothing, and a
      wash laid inside it while a control is held. These four pickers set how far
      each of those two tones sits from the page behind it, separately for light
      and dark appearance, because the two have opposite headroom (the paper is 4
      levels off white, the dark field is 18 off black).

      The scale is signed and shared by all four. 0 means the tone EQUALS the
      field — 1:1, i.e. nothing is drawn. Negative is darker than the field,
      positive is lighter, and +/-9 is the end of the gamut (black or white).
      Each row is labelled with its real measured contrast ratio against that
      appearance's field, so 3:1 is a row you can pick rather than a number to
      work out. The shipped tones are the +/-1 rows and are the defaults.
    -->
    <dict>
      <key>Type</key>
      <string>PSGroupSpecifier</string>
      <key>Title</key>
      <string>Button Pad — Light Appearance</string>
      <key>FooterText</key>
      <string>Contrast against the page, measured. 0 draws nothing; 3:1 meets WCAG 1.4.11 for an unlabelled control. The lighter-than-the-page rows exist for symmetry and are close to useless: the paper is already near white, so they span four tones between 1.00:1 and 1.03:1 and several of them repeat.</string>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Type</key>
      <string>PSMultiValueSpecifier</string>
      <key>Title</key>
      <string>Outline Contrast</string>
      <key>Key</key>
      <string>padOutlineContrastLight</string>
      <key>DefaultValue</key>
      <integer>-1</integer>
      <key>Values</key>
      <array>
        <integer>-9</integer>
        <integer>-8</integer>
        <integer>-7</integer>
        <integer>-6</integer>
        <integer>-5</integer>
